WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:01.340
Lucas:
It's like a sunset.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:03.220
Of tuna.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.130
The producers told me that I was going to be
interacting and eating sushi in some way.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.660
The rarity, the specialness of the experience
was not conveyed and could not have been

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.700
conveyed. Here we are in Tokyo.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.200
Just got off the plane a couple of hours
ago.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.060
I was supposed to be on a beach in Bali, but
National Geographic called the other day and

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.130
said, uh, cut your vacation short, come to
Tokyo.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.490
And now I don't know what the plan is going
to be.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.610
It's a letter and it's a list of sorts,
sorted into categories.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:42.710
It says Dear Lucas and a residential
neighborhood in Tokyo, Chef Hisashi Otatsu

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.270
presents an omakase menu with some creative
and non-traditional ingredients and cooking

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:50.540
methods that put it on our 2024 best of the
world list.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.440
Your mission. Find out why by completing our
scavenger hunt.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.590
Don't forget to take photos as you complete
each task.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.740
First things first, test out the camera.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:01.760
Selfie.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.280
Mean mug. We're actually doing this now.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.850
First is to find Utsutsu sushi.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.700
Ride the subway to Nakameguro station.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.760
Ride the subway.

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:23.330
Done. Walk along the canal and explore the
neighborhood.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.780
Done. Bring a present to give to the owner
if you don't have one, purchase one.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.990
Baked goods might be kind of cute.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.080
A haircut. Maybe some tea.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.500
Got it. I'm holding the tea underneath my
armpit.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:43.880
You just can't see it.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.720
Done. After the train tracks.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.920
They said you'd see it when you see it.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:57.610
Find that sushi.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:04.860
Done. I think there's something magical when
chefs meet each other, mostly because most

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.760
chefs are so kind and so nice and know what
we go through within the industry.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.690
But more than anything, we're also
perpetual, lifelong students.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.580
Wherever we're eating, whatever we're eating,
whoever we're eating with huge objective of

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:24.780
ours is to understand the deliciousness.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:29.700
I felt very much at home and at ease with
chef because he was so happy to, to teach me

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:35.760
things that I didn't know. This I'm sorry to
ask this, but can I take a photo of you?

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.920
Done.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:50.190
I cooked in Japan a while ago in kaiseki, but
because I was very junior, I was not allowed

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:55.590
to touch fish. So this is like a very next
level thing for me.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.840
As a chef, I don't think I'm ever going to
get this opportunity ever again to sit in a

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.280
restaurant with a chef and ask so many
probing questions and meet a chef who is so

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.940
open to answering those questions and
genuinely helping me understand a little bit

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:27.510
more about the evolution and the movement of
sushi through the ages.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:45.810
Mhm. This is ridiculous.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.680
It's so baller to start a meal with texture
that you don't anticipate when you walk

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.130
inside of such a serene omakase sushi place.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.460
As a chef who's cooked in the western world,
you see microgreens, you get scared because

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.050
you think that it's going to be bitter.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.520
It's going to be it's going to take things
away.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:08.910
But when it's doubled down on that in the
center to such texture and the oiliness of

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:15.060
the fish plays with the natural bitterness
inside of those greens, it's such an

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:18.450
unexpected first step into a new direction.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.540
I've never started any sushi meal like this.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:32.250
First bite of the meal done.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:44.800
What.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.050
The imprint on there isn't just for show,
it's actually Smokey.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.470
I feel like it's a call out to some of the
smokey flavors that are going to happen

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:59.660
later. When we look at a restaurant like
sushi, a lot of people want to throw the word

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.450
experimental and new on there.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:06.260
But in our conversation with chef, one of
the most important words he said was new

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:29.120
tradition. So how would you describe your
style of sushi?

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.400
Learn about his cooking philosophy.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:47.840
Done.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.680
It's kind of smoke, I think.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:01.280
Mhm. God damn it!

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:06.380
Sometimes I wish I was acting, but I
genuinely am not.

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.850
First of all, I've never had wasabi like
that before.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.550
Why isn't it spicy?

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.720
I was reminded that that's how wasabi tastes
like all around the world.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.130
Most wasabi that you get in takeout sushi
restaurants is actually horseradish that's

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.800
dyed green. And so it's got an
aggressiveness to it.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.180
One of the cool things that chef did was
bring in his artisan that had made a lot of

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:38.330
his chopsticks table settings, in this case
specifically the wasabi grater.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:42.290
So what is this? What is it for?

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.300
And why do we have to make it out of the
stingray.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:17.840
Do you see that your philosophies match up?

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.950
Ask about his ingredients and tools, done.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:32.570
And Udatsu san was nice enough to show us
that process and to let me try my hand at it.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.840
Oh, it's so, um.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.190
It's very green.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:40.490
It's very vegetal.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:46.940
The spice is momentary and it's exciting and
it's explosive, but it's not excessive.

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.520
Grate Fresh wasabi, done.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:08:05.100
So I see lotus roots.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.200
Particularly excited about this as a Chinese
chef, because there's a Chinese ingredient of

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.490
a Chinese pickled mustard greens.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:16.590
But when we saw the prep of this earlier,
it's a totally different, um, color.

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.600
It's bright green instead of yellow.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:23.970
I'm expecting a lot of crunchiness, a little
bit of that, like silky sliminess from the

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:28.260
nagaimo and importantly, the first
appearance of rice in this tasting menu.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:34.470
Mhm. Wow.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.140
I love this.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:44.580
Actually out of this world.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:50.580
The focus that chef has, plus the restraint
he has to bring in new elements.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.710
There's something new going on there.

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.950
Okay, here we go. First piece of nigiri.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:08:58.640
Chef Udatsu:
Nigiri.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:12.920
Lucas:
Yummy. A beautiful thing that I learned about
sushi today is that if the fish is good, the

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:20.030
fish part is good, but the rice is perhaps
the most reflective of the sense of style of

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:21.320
each sushi chef.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:34.730
What is your approach to the rice here?

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.260
It was really special that we got the chance
to see how that rice was prepared.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:45.050
What you can hear now is the rice alarm,
which basically is the rice jumping up and

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.510
down inside of the pot, causing the lid to
clank.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:57.550
Sushi chef Udatsu makes two types of rice,
one for maguro that's a little bit more

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:00.850
punchy, a little bit more acidic, and the
other for everything that's not mackerel.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.060
It's such like minute differences.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:08.830
But from the time the rice gets arrest to
the manipulation of the rice, all changes the

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:10.240
texture just so slightly.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.750
So that when we're tasting it like this, it
doesn't taste like how it did with the fish.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.700
But eventually throughout the process, it
will.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:18.550
Preparing the two types of rice, done.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:26.420
Something really interesting about a lot of
the things I've had today is the sequence of

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:29.510
flavor and the evolution, and like the
timeline within the palette.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:33.530
Mhm. Like something will come in and there'll
be one texture and then there'll be like a

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:34.940
hidden little surprise in the middle.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:36.200
So good.

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.720
In this case a sweet small burst of sweet
shrimp in the center.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:46.070
I haven't seen before and I didn't know it
was possible.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:53.630
Learn how to cut fish in the traditional
Japanese style.

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:54.860
We did not do that.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:59.690
It would have been inappropriate, given how
woefully insufficient my knife skills are.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:10.070
However, we did learn a lot about fish
butchery and everything to do with the fish.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:24.340
One of the most amazing things as a chef
watching a sushi chef is that every single

00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:26.320
step is so neat.

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:28.360
Everything is squared off.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:43.540
Very aspirational stuff.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.610
To clear the air as opposed.

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:51.740
I don't speak Japanese anymore.

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:57.440
I worked in Japan for a couple of years,
picked up a kitchen level Japanese which is

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:06.710
50% grunting, and then lost the 50% of
grammatical syntactical Japanese due to, uh,

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:09.050
an ex-girlfriend telling me that my Japanese
is bad.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.890
Learn how to cut fish in the traditional
Japanese style.

00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:14.060
Sort of done.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:18.020
The chef also brought a second artisan.

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:28.970
He is the go to knife expert for all of the
top sushi restaurants in all of Tokyo.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:39.590
What's incredible about that relationship is
how well he's gotten to know each chef that

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:40.610
he serves.

00:12:41.120 --> 00:13:09.800
How do you talk about knives and how do you
find the right knife for the right chef?

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:27.990
Meet the knife guy, done.

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:39.460
Nothing prepared me for this.

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:44.290
Everything just collapses into a puddle of
maximal flavor.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:48.250
The fat just melts and pushes on, which is
like into a puddle all over your palate.

00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:51.940
Then the rice starts to come up into those
little pieces that you chew through.

00:13:52.570 --> 00:14:01.030
Little pop pop up, up, up, too good.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:07.420
Wah! The crab is really nice and sweet.

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:16.760
Yum. Try sushi from a company that it's not
from Udatsu Sushi as a point of comparison.

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:27.880
Okay, so we found a negitoro, which is a
minced tuna with chives.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.580
Mass produced nori has this, like brilliant
green color, but it just doesn't have that

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.210
crisp, obviously, that Udatsu sushi had.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.070
The rice doesn't have the same amount of
chew.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:42.690
It's a little bit gluey.

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:47.970
And the fish, obviously, in order to keep it
preserved inside of a cold case, it doesn't

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.490
have the same texture and actually doesn't
taste very much like fish.

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:51.900
Not to say that this tastes bad.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.180
Just doesn't compare.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:56.220
Oh my goodness, I didn't take a photo.

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:00.060
This photo is missing but I swear I did it.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:19.320
Taste the convenience store sushi done.

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:27.240
What? One bite.

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:38.360
The layers of flavor again usually when you
when I've had sushi before and we get to uni,

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:41.480
they always ask would you like this one or
that one, this type or that type?

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:43.040
This has both.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:44.420
One a little bit more textural and buttery.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:45.920
The other one, Punchy.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:47.510
Punchy, bright and sweet.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.790
Crisp from the fried seaweed in the middle.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.560
And then that signature rice.

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:55.340
This is the part of the meal when everything
starts to blend together.

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:58.610
Because as the flow picks up, the rhythm
picks up.

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:01.190
Everything's just happening.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:10.720
And all our job as customers is to try to
grasp what's happening to us

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:23.510
. I'm sensitive to the word fusion because it
implies a sort of crass bombardment of

00:16:23.510 --> 00:16:29.420
cuisine and a mish mash of things in a way
that is done in pursuit of novelty instead of

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:31.790
pursuit of genuine deliciousness.

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.220
Whoa! I'm so sorry.

00:16:34.250 --> 00:16:35.570
Can someone translate what's happening right
now?

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:41.070
Prawn roll infused into egg yolk sauce.

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:44.910
Is that what it is? Tradition informs chefs
evolution.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:49.080
But what keeps him going is deliciousness.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:57.030
And there are also moments of that meal that
are just so, like purification level of this

00:16:57.030 --> 00:17:00.840
really, really, really delicious things put
together, like that egg yolk sauce with a

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:02.160
little bit of rice left in the bottom of the
bowl.

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:08.760
Wow. But I don't want to discount the amount
of work that chef has put into understanding

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:13.050
an older form of sushi upon which he is
building sushi.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:30.670
What? So what the hell is going on?

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.870
What the hell is that?

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:41.040
Look at this. I don't know if you guys can
see it.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:44.690
It's like a sunset, of tuna.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:45.380
Chef Udatsu:
One bite.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:48.380
I will try.

00:17:56.500 --> 00:18:01.960
Lucas:
I could try to say something poetic, like,
oh, this is the bite that encapsulates all

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:06.040
these other bites that preceded it, but that
is just I mean, that's a ridiculous thing.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:09.430
You can't not experience that to know what
that feels like.

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:13.030
Oh.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:20.050
Wow. What a climax.

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:25.630
Oh, hey. Whoa.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.210
That's got plenty of fish in it, but it
teases out all that sweetness.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.120
It's fun. And it's funny.

00:18:34.120 --> 00:18:40.180
Egg for dessert. Like this. Very good.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:45.270
I think I need a moment to process this.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.920
The best tasting menus feel like a journey,
because not only have you gone from point A

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:54.090
to point B like you've physically moved, but
then you can look back at every single slice

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.980
of that experience and remember it and
reflect on it and feel like it all

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:03.240
accumulated to something like A plus B, plus
C, and it all like maximized into this big

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:08.550
thing. And that's my feeling, especially
with that last Maguro Futomaki and then the

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:16.860
the Tamago.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:19.860
Thank you.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.970
On this list, there are two things that it
asks of me.

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:34.970
One is to have a Zen moment, and the second
is to write a haiku about my experience with

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:39.530
my utter lack of command of the Japanese
language and a little bit of help from our

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:41.630
friendly crew members.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:43.790
I present to you the following.

00:19:54.250 --> 00:19:55.660
That's pretty good. Five.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:58.970
Seven. Five. Done.

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:06.650
The takeaway I think from today is that there
is no sense of progress without deep

00:20:06.650 --> 00:20:08.180
understanding of tradition.

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:12.140
And Udatsu Sushi is a shining example of
that.

00:20:25.450 --> 00:20:29.380
At the end of the meal, chef and I had a
drink at the bar next door at Bar Rokusho.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:35.050
Cheers.

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:36.340
Chef Udatsu:
Thank you very much.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:41.870
Lucas:
Thank you. How do you feel?

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:51.290
After cooking like that.

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:58.880
I bet if I was cooking all day, and this guy
made me keep talking about the philosophy of

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.980
food, I just want to, like, "shut up.

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:05.630
Can we talk about baseball or something?"
You know.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:08.720
Chef Udatsu:
Finish. Yeah.

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:09.710
Just drink.

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:11.910
Lucas:
Done.

